/-! # Affineness descends along finite surjective covers of Noetherian algebraic spaces

Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Cohomology, Tag 07VP,
lemma-image-affine-finite-morphism-affine-Noetherian
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-space-finite-affine-descent).

The three hypotheses on X express local Noetherianity, quasi-compactness and absolute
quasi-separatedness. Finiteness is tested on all scheme base changes; surjectivity uses
field-valued point classes. The representing affine scheme retains its morphism to S.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A Noetherian algebraic space covered finitely and surjectively by an affine scheme
is represented by an affine scheme over its given base.
Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Cohomology, Tag 07VP
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-space-finite-affine-descent).

Proof:
1. Put P(F) := H¹(X,F) = 0 on coherent modules, using underlying additive sheaves.
   Grothendieck abelian sheafification supplies the module-forgetful lemma's hypotheses.
   The long exact sequence proves extension stability and P(0); functoriality proves
   invariance under isomorphism. Inclusion into, and projection from, any positive finite
   direct sum exhibit H¹(X,F) as a retract, proving the required summand condition.
2. For a reduced irreducible nonempty closed subspace i : Z → X, base change the finite
   surjection to f' : Z' → Z. The finite-cover structure-support lemma makes
   G := f'_* O_Z' coherent with full support. The other projection Z' → Y is a closed
   immersion; testing with Y itself shows Z' is a scheme. The closed-immersion quotient
   description then makes Z' affine, without assuming Z is a scheme.
3. For a nonzero quasi-coherent ideal I of O_Z, let I' be the ideal generated by its
   image on Z'. The affine ideal-product projection lemma gives G' := IG = f'_* I'.
   This uses the image ideal, not injectivity of the pullback of I → O_Z.
4. Noetherian coherent-subquotient and support results make G', G/G' and O_Z/I coherent
   with closed support. The support of G/G' lies in V(I). This closed set is proper:
   a nonzero section on a reduced affine étale chart is not nilpotent, so some prime
   avoids it; at a geometric point above that prime the ideal is the unit ideal.
5. Zariski–étale quasi-coherent equivalence on the affine scheme Z' identifies I' with
   the étale sheaf of a quasi-coherent Zariski module. Affine étale acyclicity gives
   H¹(Z',I') = 0. Affine-map cohomology comparison, first for f' and then for the closed
   immersion i, gives H¹(Z,G') = 0 and H¹(X,i_* G') = 0.
6. The coherent devissage criterion applies using steps 1–5 and gives P(F) for every
   coherent F on X, with the higher-rank ideal-product condition satisfied by G' = IG.
7. Every quasi-coherent F is the filtered union of its coherent submodules. The
   module-forgetful functor preserves this colimit, by its sheafification lemma. Since
   X is quasi-compact and quasi-separated, filtered-colimit cohomology identifies
   H¹(X,F) with the colimit of the vanishing coherent H¹ groups. Thus it also vanishes.
8. The algebraic-space Serre affineness criterion now represents X by an affine scheme;
   its representing object over S carries the original structural morphism to S. -/
theorem exists_affine_of_noetherian_finite_surjective
    {S : Scheme.{u}} (X : AlgebraicSpace S)
    (hln : ∀ T : X.EtaleSite, IsLocallyNoetherian (X.etaleForget.obj T))
    (hqc : CompactSpace (Point X.obj.obj)) (hqs : IsAbsolutelyQuasiSeparated X)
    (Y : Over S) [IsAffine Y.left] (f : yoneda.obj Y ⟶ X.obj.obj)
    (hfin : IsRepresentableWith (fun _ _ p ↦ IsFinite p.left) f)
    (hsurj : pointImage f = Set.univ) :
    ∃ T : Over S, IsAffine T.left ∧ Nonempty (yoneda.obj T ≅ X.obj.obj) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
